{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>And Moses brought the people to meet Elohim.</b> The implication is that the Shekhinah went first and came forth to meet them, and so Rashi explained — in agreement with what is concluded in the Yalkut in Jeremiah (Jeremiah 30, remez 521) on the verse ״מִי זֶה עָרַב לָגֶשֶׁת״ (Jeremiah 30:21). Rabba bar Rav Huna said: this is an extra distinction for Israel over converts, for concerning Israel it is written (Jeremiah 31:32) ״וְהָיִיתִי לָהֶם לֵאלֹהִים״ and afterward ״וְהֵמָּה יִהְיוּ לִי לְעָם״, whereas concerning converts it is written (ibid. 30:21) ״מִי הוּא זֶה עָרַב אֶת לִבּוֹ לָגֶשֶׁת אֵלַי״ and afterward ״וִהְיִיתֶם לִי לְעָם וְאָנֹכִי אֶהְיֶה לָכֶם לֵאלֹהִים״. And Rashi explained: like a bridegroom who goes forth to meet the bride, etc. — for who pursues after whom? The owner of the lost object pursues after his lost object. So too the Holy One, blessed be He, pursues after Israel, who were like a lost flock among the nations, as it is written (Hosea 9:10) ״כַּעֲנָבִים בַּמִּדְבָּר מָצָאתִי אֲבוֹתֵיכֶם״, etc." "<b>And they stood at the lower part of the mountain.</b> From here the Rabbis of blessed memory learned (Shabbat 88a) that He held the mountain over them like a cask, saying: if you do not accept the Torah, there shall be your grave. And Rav Aḥa bar Ya'akov said: from here there is a great protestation concerning the Torah, for Israel are able to say, 'We were coerced.' The explanation is not that HaShem wished to coerce them in order to give them a ready excuse — for if so, when He went around to all the nations and they did not wish to accept the Torah, why did He not force them? And furthermore, Israel had already accepted it with good will and said ״כֹּל אֲשֶׁר דִּבֶּר ה׳ נַעֲשֶׂה״ (Exodus 19:8). And moreover, in the days of Ezekiel, when they wished to cast off from themselves the yoke of the kingdom of heaven and said ״נִהְיֶה כַגּוֹיִם״, HaShem answered them ״כִּי בְּחֵמָה שְׁפוּכָה אֶמְלוֹךְ עֲלֵיכֶם״ (Ezekiel 20:32–33) — and had they been coerced in accepting the Torah, how could He have forced them? Rather, it is certain that for their own good He held the mountain over them, saying: see that as long as you do not accept the Torah, you are regarded as dead even in your lifetime, and in your death you have no remnant at all — there shall be your grave, ״וּמֵתִים בַּל יִחְיוּ רְפָאִים בַּל יָקוּמוּ״ (Isaiah 26:14). But through the Torah your lives will be life in this world and in the world to come. And Rav Aḥa bar Ya'akov said: even though He did not wish to coerce them regarding acceptance of the Torah, nevertheless a harm resulted from this for the generations to come, who would err by saying, 'We were coerced,' and this was their claim in the days of Ezekiel. And in the days of Ahasuerus they accepted what they had already upheld (Esther 9:27) — that is, they took it upon themselves no longer to advance the claim of coercion, for that claim is a claim in error." "And one may say further: ״שָׁם תִּהְיֶה קְבוּרַתְכֶם״ — that you would not merit to enter the land of Israel, for they were promised the inheritance of the nations on condition that they keep His statutes. And He specified burial in particular, because the dead of the land of Israel live first [at the resurrection], and He said to them: if you do not accept the Torah, then surely you have no portion in the resurrection of the dead for the world to come, and then there is no difference between burial in the land of Israel and outside the land. Therefore ״בַּמִּדְבָּר תָּמֻתוּ וְשָׁם תִּקָּבֵרוּ״ — for what difference does it make if they also carry up your bones to be buried there, if you have no portion in the world to come?"], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {1 "The Kli Yakar attributes the statement 'מִכָּאן מוֹדָעָא רַבָּא לְאוֹרָיְיתָא' to Rav Aḥa bar Ya'akov following the Gemara in Shabbat 88a; the repetition of his name introduces the Kli Yakar's own resolution of the difficulty."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:21:36.816012764Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}